WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.290
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:09.629
This paper is about creating high-quality
physics simulations and is, in my opinion,

00:00:09.629 --> 00:00:12.019
one of the gems very few people know about.

00:00:12.019 --> 00:00:17.830
In these physical simulations, we have objects
that undergo a lot of tormenting, for instance

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:24.259
they have to endure all kinds of deformations,
rotations, and of course, being pushed around.

00:00:24.259 --> 00:00:29.689
A subset of these simulation techniques requires
us to be able to look at these deformations

00:00:29.689 --> 00:00:33.870
and forget about anything they do other than
the rotational part.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:38.090
Don’t push it, don’t squish it, just take
the rotational part.

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:43.530
Here, the full deformation transform is shown
with red, and extracted rotational part is

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:46.080
shown by the green indicators here.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.100
This problem is not particularly hard and
has been studied for decades, so we have excellent

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:56.550
solutions to this, for instance, techniques
that we refer to as polar decomposition, singular

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:58.900
value decomposition, and more.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:03.850
By the way, in our earlier project together
with the Activision Blizzard company, we also

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:09.510
used the singular value decomposition to compute
the scattering of light within our skin and

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:10.990
other translucent materials.

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:14.620
I’ve put a link in the video description,
make sure to have a look!

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:20.170
Okay, so if a bunch of techniques already
exist to perform this, why do we need to invent

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:21.500
anything here?

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:25.880
Why make a video about something that has
been solved many decades ago?

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:32.479
Well, here’s why: we don’t have anything
yet that is criterion one, robust, which means

00:01:32.479 --> 00:01:35.200
that it works perfectly all the time.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.260
Even a slight inaccuracy is going to make
an object implode our simulations, so we better

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:41.909
get something that is robust.

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:47.440
And, since these physical simulations are
typically implemented on the graphics card,

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:54.350
criterion two, we need something that is well
suited for that, and is as simple as possible.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:58.870
It turns out, none of the existing techniques
check both of these two boxes.

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:03.800
If you start reading the paper, you will see
a derivation of this new solution, a mathematical

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:07.390
proof that it is true and works all the time.

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:12.680
And then, as an application, it shows fun
physical simulations that utilize this technique.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:18.090
You can see here that these simulations are
stable, no objects are imploding, although

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:22.749
this extremely drunk dragon is showing a formidable
attempt at doing that.

00:02:22.749 --> 00:02:23.749
Ouch.

00:02:23.749 --> 00:02:28.420
All the contortions and movements are modeled
really well over a long time frame, and the

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:33.329
original shape of the dragon can be recovered
without any significant numerical errors.

00:02:33.329 --> 00:02:39.659
Finally, it also compares the source code
for a previous method, and, the new method.

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:45.040
As you see, there is a vast difference in
terms of complexity that favors the new method.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:50.349
It is short, does not involve a lot of branching
decisions and is therefore an excellent candidate

00:02:50.349 --> 00:02:53.129
to run on state of the art graphics cards.

00:02:53.129 --> 00:02:57.499
What I really like in this paper is that it
does not present something and claims that

00:02:57.499 --> 00:02:59.980
“well, this seems to work”.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:05.200
It first starts out with a crystal clear problem
statement that is impossible to misunderstand.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:10.519
Then, the first part of the a paper is pure
mathematics, proves the validity of a new

00:03:10.519 --> 00:03:16.230
technique, and then, drops it into a physical
simulation, showing that it is indeed what

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:17.550
we were looking for.

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:22.840
And finally, a super simple piece of source
code is provided so anyone can use it almost

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:23.920
immediately.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.430
This is one of the purest computer graphics
papers out there I’ve seen in a while.

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:30.599
Make sure to have a look in the video description.

00:03:30.599 --> 00:03:33.450
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

